# Nothing to grant here, and the reason is worth keeping.
#
# Android 10 stopped letting most processes open /dev/ashmem and put a
# service in front of it that hands out a descriptor instead. libcutils asks
# for that service first, so the allocator asks too, and is denied finding
# it -- seventy times over a boot.
#
# The rule for it cannot be written: ashmem_device_service is a framework
# type and vendor policy cannot name it. That is the treble split doing its
# job rather than an omission. A vendor process is not meant to reach the
# framework's servicemanager at all, and libcutils knows it -- when the
# lookup fails it opens the device, which the allocator is allowed to do.
#
# So the denials are the fallback working as designed, and the file exists
# so that the next person counting them does not write the rule and find out
# the hard way.

# gralloc.nvgpu keeps each buffer's shared metadata in a memfd of its own,
# made by the allocator (nvgralloc-shared) and so labelled with its tmpfs
# type, and every process that maps the buffer maps that file too: whoever
# draws, the composer and surfaceflinger. AOSP's own platform keeps the
# metadata in the dma-buf and grants nothing like this; device/linaro's
# dragonboard, whose gralloc does the same as this one, grants it to the
# same clients. map is checked from 4.13 on, not by this kernel.
allow {
  appdomain
  bootanim
  hal_graphics_composer_server
  surfaceflinger
  system_server
} hal_graphics_allocator_default_tmpfs:file { getattr read write map };

# An app runs with categories of its own and the file has none, so an app
# may not write it whatever the rule above says: the MLS constraint on write
# wants the levels equal. The file is shared across every app by design.
typeattribute hal_graphics_allocator_default_tmpfs mlstrustedobject;
